hi there steve kaufman here  um one of the uh i guess common themes  here at uh my  video uh my channel here youtube and  also on my blog is the  rather spectacular lack of success of  formal language instruction in schools  and we spend a lot of time  and a lot of money on instructing kids  in languages at school  and certainly here in canada  english-speaking school children very  few of them end up being able to speak  french although they all have to take  french  in a previous video i talked about the  experience in japan where  kids spend i don't know how many hours  on english  and they don't  learn very well those that learn well  are the ones that are motivated and  learn on their own  uh and i've sort of come to the  conclusion that the biggest problem the  biggest reason why if we had to find one  reason why kids have a tough time with  language learning at school i would say  it's because of the tests and i include  in tests all kinds of exercises tests  wherever the teacher tries to test the  knowledge of the learner  you know those kids that have their  first experience with acquiring another  language or learning another language  outside the school system either from  their mother if say the  mother speaks a different language from  the father or because they move to a  different country  those kids learn very well and they end  up very often speaking another language  whereas the ones that  encounter the language in a in a school  setting i end up not speaking very well  at all  and you know i looked at some of the  comments i had in japan about the kinds  of things they do  in class where they decode texts and and  of course i had a comment from one of my  uh viewers who is a teacher in japan who  explained that they have to do such  things as write out  um you know the same word 400 times but  all because they're trying to learn  these words for a specific test so  everything is geared around the test so  they make the kids do things that are  ridiculous  like writing words out 400 times  specifically for the test  um you know and i look at my uh  my granddaughter who had to recite a  poem now there again it's not for a test  but it was a poem that she wasn't  interested in it wasn't a very  interesting poem she was there trying to  memorize it so that the next day should  repeat it in class in other words the  teacher is looking for some  demonstration  that the kids have learned  and then we contrast that with the  example i gave of the teacher in algeria  who had his students  learn the words of popular songs like  the beatles  and i've heard that kind of thing before  i was  again at this french language meetup  there was another lady there vancouver  anglophone who spoke english spoke  french very well and i asked her why she  did so well and she said well we had  so-and-so as a teacher in such and such  a school and everybody knows that  whoever had so and so  they all learned to speak french well  well i said what did she do well she got  us interested in the you know  felix le claire and and some of the you  know well-known singers from quebec  uh  no that meant everybody's taste but she  managed to make that interesting so she  made it interesting for her students so  you know i get back this thing there are  so many tools available today  if we forget about testing the kids  if the objective is like how do we make  it fun how do we make sure that they  spend time with the language and some  will do better and some will do worse  we  we just have to find something that  turns the crank  and i've mentioned this before when i  described how i would run a classroom i  would let them read in class i would let  them read and and i would give them you  know everyone i really have to say i  think this the ipod and maybe there's  other brand names that are just as good  but the ipod touch is just an amazing  tool  it's an amazing tool because you can put  so much in the way of learning resources  dictionaries texts  flash card system you name it into this  little device and we're only just  beginning and if this cost two hundred  dollars every kid should get one because  it costs the school system  something like seven thousand dollars to  put each kid through  school per year  so buying them one of these things if  they don't already have them is a  relatively small amount of money  compared to the amount that is wasted  uh largely through this uh school system  with all the excessive bureaucracy and  meetings and professional professional  days for the teachers and all the rest  of it  and i would have them reading class  and i've been reading now myself i've  been reading uh in italian  uh using the dictionary that i have on  on my ipod  and then i collect all and of course it  collects in a list and then i take that  list of words and i punch it all into  link so that as i load up more content  on link all of these words are already  in my database highlighted in yellow and  so forth and so on it's delightful and  it's fun to do it's fun to sit there and  look things up in the dictionary so i  would have them doing that i would  expose them to things of interest i mean  obviously you'd have to mix up  some freedom of choice to for them to  listen to things that they want to do  and some things you'd have to do in  common so that in class you could talk  about it i would have them as i've said  before i would have the kids  select their favorite words and phrases  from their listening and reading and  maybe  talk about them in class initially in in  their own language  and eventually uh if if some are ready  for it to talk in the target language  but it doesn't have to be that  i think it should just be a matter of of  getting them somehow hoping to get them  turned on to the language because  there's one thing that's for sure and  that is that the present system with the  emphasis on testing and testing trying  to prove that they've learned what we  taught them  and and doing that year after year  uh when we see that in the end the  result is that very few of them can  speak the language at least in the case  of english canada  and in japan  only the very motivated learn  i think the system is the situation is  different in certain countries in europe  not in southern europe uh to judge from  my discussions with albert in barcelona  and with alberto in italy  uh the situation there is the same as in  canada and japan  now in sweden the situation is different  but i think that has nothing to do with  the amount of testing to do at school  and has everything to do with the fact  that  so many television programs are  available in english and the the kids  there are exposed to english-speaking  culture whether from england or from the  united states  uh you know as as early uh you know in  in their childhood and they're exposed  to english-speaking pop culture and  whatever it might be so  the message here is you know the more i  think of it i i'm just convinced that  that is test you know this desire  it's not as like let's face it language  learning in ours if i take the case of  english-speaking canadians  we basically assume the kids are not  gonna are not gonna learn to speak  french i mean let's let's get serious  uh  for the last i'm you know 64  uh you know for at least 50 years nobody  learns french at school so that it's  basically a given that they're not going  to learn through this pro through this  process so i think we can afford to  experiment a little and having seen the  results of the japanese english speaking  instruction uh program  you know i i think there should be some  room for for experimentation because  what what's happening right now doesn't  work it's like  it's not doing the job i mean that's a  given i think learning these languages  almost becomes like a luxury it's  something that's on the program and we  kind of have to do it but nobody really  expects anybody to learn  we do expect people to learn how to add  and subtract we do expect people to  learn how to read and write  obviously people who want to study  science and so forth they have to learn  certain  you know the foundations of those of  those disciplines in math and physics  but in language that doesn't matter the  foundations don't matter  you can learn  certain vocabulary or or certain  basic structures later there i don't  believe that it's that language is a  matter of building creating a foundation  and building on that foundation i'm  totally uh convinced that that's not the  case at all it's it's more like a a  crossword puzzle that as you put more  pieces in there it starts to become  clearer gradually and you don't know at  what point certain things are going to  become clear and it's only through this  through this exposure so  if we can somehow interest kids in the  language  and let them just acquire words and  familiarity with the language  they can't do worse than what's  happening right now  so  they're  as i say i think we should get rid of  tests in the school system and so far as  language instruction is concerned gone  none period i think we'll get better  results  okay thanks for listening

